{"schema": 1, "ok": true, "kind": "pde", "pde": "disk", "order": 2, "indep": "x", "dep": "u", "input_latex": "\\nabla^2 u = 0,\\quad r < 1;\\quad u(1,\\theta) = u\\left(\\theta\\right)", "normal_latex": "\\nabla^2 u = 0,\\quad r < 1;\\quad u(1,\\theta) = u\\left(\\theta\\right)", "rhs_expr": null, "ic": null, "domain": {"x_min": -1.0, "x_max": 1.0, "y_min": -1.0, "y_max": 1.0, "t_max": 0.0, "u_min": 0.0, "u_max": 1.0, "mask": "disk"}, "initial_expr": null, "surface": true, "summary": "Laplace's equation inside a circle: the steady temperature for the given boundary values. The graph shows it as a heat map over the disk.", "other_types": [], "methods": [{"id": "separation", "name": "Separation of variables", "applicable": true, "reason": "In polar coordinates the boundary is a coordinate line, and periodicity in the angle supplies the eigenvalue problem.", "steps": [{"title": "Write the equation in polar coordinates", "text": "On a disk the boundary is \\(r = R\\), so use \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\) and look for product solutions \\(u = F(r)\\,G(\\theta)\\).", "latex": ["u_{rr} + \\frac{1}{r}u_r + \\frac{1}{r^2}u_{\\theta\\theta} = 0", "\\frac{r^2F'' + rF'}{F} = -\\frac{G''}{G} = \\lambda"], "substeps": []}, {"title": "The angular problem", "text": "\\(G\\) must repeat when \\(\\theta\\) increases by \\(2\\pi\\). That periodicity is the boundary condition, and it forces \\(\\lambda = n^2\\).", "latex": ["G'' + \\lambda G = 0,\\qquad G(\\theta + 2\\pi) = G(\\theta)", "\\lambda_n = n^2,\\qquad G_n = \\cos n\\theta,\\ \\sin n\\theta \\quad (n = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots)"], "substeps": []}, {"title": "The radial problem", "text": "The radial equation is a Cauchy\u2013Euler equation with solutions \\(r^n\\) and \\(r^{-n}\\) (or \\(1\\) and \\(\\ln r\\) for \\(n = 0\\)). The ones that blow up at the centre are discarded, because the temperature there is finite.", "latex": ["r^2F'' + rF' - n^2F = 0", "F_n = r^n"], "substeps": []}, {"title": "Superpose", "text": "Add the product solutions. Setting \\(r = R\\) shows that the constants are the Fourier coefficients of the boundary values.", "latex": ["u(r,\\theta) = \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(\\frac{r}{R}\\right)^n\\left(a_n\\cos n\\theta + b_n\\sin n\\theta\\right)", "a_n = \\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(\\theta)\\cos n\\theta\\,d\\theta,\\qquad b_n = \\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(\\theta)\\sin n\\theta\\,d\\theta"], "substeps": []}, {"title": "Compute the coefficients", "text": "Here \\(f(\\theta) = u\\left(\\theta\\right)\\) for \\(-\\pi < \\theta < \\pi\\). The coefficients are listed by value (only the nonzero ones are shown).", "latex": ["a_0 = 1"], "substeps": [], "table": {"headers": ["n", "a_n", "b_n"], "rows": [["1", "0", "0.63662"], ["3", "0", "0.212207"], ["5", "0", "0.127324"], ["7", "0", "0.090946"], ["9", "0", "0.070736"], ["11", "0", "0.057875"], ["13", "0", "0.048972"], ["15", "0", "0.042442"]]}}, {"title": "The value at the centre", "text": "At \\(r = 0\\) only the constant term survives, so the temperature at the centre is the average of the boundary values.", "latex": ["u(0,\\theta) = \\frac{a_0}{2} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(\\theta)\\,d\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}"], "substeps": []}, {"title": "Poisson's integral formula", "text": "Summing the series in closed form gives the solution as a weighted average of the boundary values.", "latex": ["u(r,\\theta) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\\frac{R^2 - r^2}{R^2 - 2Rr\\cos(\\theta - \\phi) + r^2}\\,f(\\phi)\\,d\\phi"], "substeps": []}, {"title": "Solution", "text": "", "latex": ["u(r,\\theta) \\approx \\frac{1}{2} + 0.63662\\,r\\sin \\theta + 0.21221\\,r^{3}\\sin 3\\theta + 0.12732\\,r^{5}\\sin 5\\theta + \\cdots"], "substeps": [], "kind": "answer"}, {"title": "Check", "text": "Checked numerically: the series satisfies the equation at sample points and the boundary conditions. With 20 terms it reproduces the boundary values closely.", "latex": [], "substeps": [], "kind": "check-ok"}], 